Reduced the work an IP Fabric sync does per object. Devices and VLANs are read in one query each rather than one per Location, creating an Interface, Location or VLAN takes one validated save rather than two, creating or deleting an object records one change log entry rather than one per write, the safe delete tag is resolved once per model rather than once per object, and objects are deleted in batches rather than one at a time.
Narrowed how Bulk Write Mode recovers from a batch the database refuses. The batch is halved and retried until the rows at fault are isolated, rather than every row in it being rewritten and revalidated one at a time, so one bad row in a batch of a thousand costs about twenty inserts instead of a thousand validated saves.
Added `ipfabric_bulk_write_batch_size`, which sets how many rows Bulk Write Mode inserts per statement. It was fixed at a thousand, which is the size of an insert and also how much has to be narrowed through when the database refuses a batch, so an estate carrying rows Nautobot refuses can now lower it.
